upplied as primitives or detailed row configs.\\n- `hiddenColumns` reserve visual space while consuming source characters.\\n- `highlightColumns` recolor glyphs without changing the face background.\\n- `rollEvenWhenSettled` forces a full alphabet cycle when the current and target character match.\\n- `animationKey` resets the settled state to blanks before replaying.\\n- `setLine` and `setLines` provide imperative per-row updates.\\n- `startWhenVisible` delays animation launch until an `IntersectionObserver` sees the board.\\n- `sound` schedules procedural Web Audio flap sounds, with browser unlock handled through `primeFlapSound`.\\n\\n## Renderer Options\\n\\nThe `renderer` prop supports five modes:\\n\\n- `dom`: default DOM/CSS `rotateX` flip with independent per-cell scheduling.\\n- `dom-batched`: same DOM visuals with frame-batched tick writes.\\n- `dom-fold`: flat fold comparison path; shipped but not recommended for performance.\\n- `canvas`: single 2D canvas with sprite caching and dirty-cell redraw.\\n- `webgl`: single canvas with a glyph atlas and batched WebGL quads; falls back to canvas when WebGL is unavailable.\\n\\nDocumentation and profiling indicate `dom` remains the compatibility-oriented default for normal boards, while `canvas` and `webgl` are the recommended dense-board renderers.\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n- `npm run build`: `vite build && tsc -p tsconfig.build.json`\\n- `npm run build:demo`: `vite build --config vite.demo.config.ts`\\n- `npm run dev`: `vite --host 127.0.0.1`\\n- `npm run preview`: `vite preview --host 127.0.0.1`\\n- `npm run test`: `pnpm build && node --test tests/*.test.mjs`\\n- `npm run test:visual`: `playwright test`\\n- `npm run typecheck`: `tsc --noEmit`\\n\\n## Test Coverage\\n\\nThe repository has Node test coverage for formatting, imperative behavior, rendering output, renderer scene geometry, sound parameters, and timeline behavior. Playwright visual coverage verifies glyph/seam alignment across all renderers, seam settings, and font sizes.\",\"architecture.md\":\"# Architecture\\n\\n## Module Layout\\n\\n- `src/index.ts`: package barrel; imports CSS and exports public component, types, timeline utilities, renderer helpers, sound APIs, and formatting helpers.\\n- `src/SplitFlapDisplay.tsx`: main React component, row normalization, DOM renderer, imperative handle, renderer selection, accessibility label generation, visibility handling, and DOM animation scheduling.\\n- `src/timeline.ts`: renderer-agnostic animation planning and sampling.\\n- `src/utils.ts`: alphabet, number, text fitting, and character lookup helpers.\\n- `src/CanvasSplitFlap.tsx`: 2D canvas renderer.\\n- `src/WebglSplitFlap.tsx`: WebGL renderer with canvas fallback.\\n- `src/renderer/useFlapDisplay.ts`: shared controller for canvas and WebGL renderers.\\n- `src/renderer/scene.ts`: shared renderer theme resolution, layout, card painting, sprite generation, and fold geometry.\\n- `src/renderer/centering.ts`: glyph centering support used to align DOM, canvas, and WebGL rendering.\\n- `src/renderer/icon.ts`: SVG icon rasterization for imperative renderers.\\n- `src/sound.ts`: procedural Web Audio flap sound engine.\\n- `src/styles.css`: DOM renderer styles and shared CSS variables.\\n\\n## Data Flow\\n\\n`SplitFlapDisplay` first normalizes props into `NormalizedRow[]`. Primitive rows become row configs, numbers are formatted with `formatSplitFlapNumber`, strings are fitted with `fitSplitFlapText`, alphabets are normalized, and hidden/highlight column indices are sorted, deduped, and range-filtered.\\n\\nThe component then applies imperative line overrides, if any. Overrides preserve the row width: numeric overrides are formatted to the row digit count, and string overrides are fitted to the existing row width. This lets `setLine` and `setLines` animate selected rows without resizing the board.\\n\\nTiming is normalized through `normalizeTiming`. If `rowStaggerMs` is not explicitly supplied, the component applies automatic row staggering only for sufficiently large boards.\\n\\nRenderer selection happens late:\\n\\n- `dom`, `dom-batched`, and `dom-fold` render rows as DOM cells and faces.\\n- `canvas` delegates to `CanvasSplitFlap`.\\n- `webgl` delegates to `WebglSplitFlap`, which probes WebGL support and falls back to canvas when needed.\\n\\n## Timeline Core\\n\\n`tickPlan` computes the forward walk through the configured alphabet from previous character to target character. It supports forced full rotations, duration padding, deceleration tails, icon tick multiplication, and icon pause gaps.\\n\\n`planCellTimeline` expands a tick plan into absolute due times for one cell. It returns the initial character, final character, all planned ticks, settle time, and completion time. Icon sentinel handling adds a short dense sequence after the sentinel so icon transitions remain readable.\\n\\n`sampleCell` is pure and renderer-agnostic. Given a cell timeline and elapsed time, it returns either a settled character or an in-progress flip with `fromChar`, `toChar`, and progress. The canvas and WebGL renderers use this on each animation frame.\\n\\n## DOM Renderer\\n\\nThe DOM path renders one row at a time. Each visible cell contains four faces: top, bottom, next-full, and next-half. Hidden columns render as spacer spans instead of flap cells. Highlighted columns are marked with `data-rsf-highlight` and styled through CSS.\\n\\n`dom` schedules each cell tick separately. `dom-batched` groups all ticks due in the same frame into a batched write. Both use the same timeline semantics; the distinction is scheduling cost, not visual behavior.\\n\\nCompletion is tracked per row. The wrapper fires `onAnimationComplete` once all rows report complete.\\n\\n## Canvas Renderer\\n\\n`CanvasSplitFlap` renders the whole board into one canvas. It resolves theme values from the wrapper's computed style, lays out cells through `layoutBoard`, caches card sprites per character, and redraws only cells whose visual signature changed. Highlighted cells are cached under separate sprite keys.\\n\\nThe renderer hosts the React icon in a hidden DOM node, rasterizes SVG icons, and bakes the icon into sprites under the icon sentinel character. Non-SVG icons are skipped rather than drawn stale.\\n\\n## WebGL Renderer\\n\\n`WebglSplitFlap` probes WebGL availability and falls back to canvas when unsupported or when initialization fails. The WebGL path builds a glyph atlas by painting card sprites into an atlas canvas and uploading it as one texture. It then emits quads into a dynamic vertex buffer and draws the whole board in a single `drawArrays` call per frame.\\n\\nHighlighted glyphs get separate atlas entries. If the atlas would exceed `MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE`, the renderer falls back to canvas to avoid corrupt output.\\n\\n## Shared Imperative Renderer Controller\\n\\n`useFlapDisplay` is the shared controller for canvas and WebGL. It handles visibility gating, previous settled values, `animationKey` resets, timeline planning, a single `requestAnimationFrame` loop, sound event emission, completion, and redraw hooks for resize/theme changes.\\n\\n## Accessibility\\n\\nAll renderers expose the same wrapper semantics: `role=\\\"img\\\"` and an `aria-label`. Visual cells/canvas contents are `aria-hidden`. Hidden columns are represented as spaces in the accessible label so consumed-but-hidden characters are not read aloud as visible content.\",\"risks.md\":\"# Risks\\n\\n## Dense DOM Performance\\n\\nThe documented performance profile shows DOM renderers degrade sharply as board size grows. At 288 cells, DOM renderers are near 60 fps with some long frames; by 800 and 1800 cells they collapse. `dom-batched` reduces scripting churn but does not change the compositor/layer scaling problem.\\n\\nMitigation: use `canvas` or `webgl` for dense boards. Keep `dom` as default for small boards and compatibility.\\n\\n## `dom-fold` Is a Comparison Path, Not a Fast Path\\n\\n`dom-fold` remains shipped but profiling shows it is slower than the 3D DOM renderer at every tested size. Consumers may misinterpret the name as an optimization.\\n\\nMitigation: documentation should continue to call out that `dom-fold` is not recommended for performance.\\n\\n## Renderer Parity\\n\\nThe project intentionally keeps timing, layout, hidden columns, highlighted columns, icon behavior, and accessibility consistent across renderers. This is a wide parity surface. Regressions can happen when adding a display feature to only the DOM path or only imperative renderers.\\n\\nMitigation: new rendering features should touch shared modules where possible (`timeline.ts`, `scene.ts`, `useFlapDisplay.ts`) and add tests across all renderer values.\\n\\n## Icon Limitations\\n\\nDOM renderers can render arbitrary React icon nodes. Canvas and WebGL rasterize SVG icons from a hidden host; non-SVG icons are skipped. This can surprise users if a custom icon works in `dom` but disappears in `canvas` or `webgl`.\\n\\nMitigation: document SVG-only behavior for imperative renderers or add a public fallback path for image/canvas-compatible icons.\\n\\n## WebGL Resource Limits\\n\\nThe WebGL renderer builds one atlas from all alphabet characters plus highlighted variants and optional icon entries. Very large alphabets, large font/cell sizes, or high DPR can exceed `MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE`; the code falls back to canvas in that case.\\n\\nMitigation: keep fallback behavior tested and avoid assuming WebGL is always active when `renderer=\\\"webgl\\\"` is requested.\\n\\n## Timing and Completion Semantics\\n\\nDOM rows track completion per row, while canvas/WebGL track one whole-board completion from the shared controller. Subtle differences may appear around no-op animations, hidden-only changes, visibility gating, or `animate={false}`.\\n\\nMitigation: tests should cover no-op completion, hidden-column behavior, visibility disabled paths, and `animationKey` replay behavior for both DOM and imperative renderers.\\n\\n## Sound Autoplay and Density\\n\\nSound depends on browser Web Audio unlock behavior and can only start after a user gesture. It is also globally rate-limited to prevent dense boards from becoming too loud. Users may think `sound` is broken if `primeFlapSound` is not called from an interaction.\\n\\nMitigation: keep README guidance explicit and keep sound scheduling decoupled from renderer animation state.\\n\\n## Build/Test Assumptions\\n\\nTests import from `dist/index.js`, so `pnpm build` must run before `node --test tests/*.test.mjs`. Visual tests depend on Playwright and ImageMagick commands (`identify`, `convert`) in `verify/alignment.spec.ts`.\\n\\nMitigation: CI should install all required system tools and run the documented scripts in order.\\n\\n## Seed Gaps\\n\\nThe seed packet skipped binary screenshots and `pnpm-lock.yaml`, and reports 29 commits without Entire checkpoint trailer coverage after the oldest exported session. Dependency resolution and historical rationale may therefore require consulting the working tree or Git history directly.\",\"maintenance-guide.md\":\"# Maintenance Guide\\n\\n## Local Workflow\\n\\nUse `pnpm` for package scripts. The primary checks are:\\n\\n```sh\\npnpm build\\npnpm test\\npnpm typecheck\\npnpm test:visual\\n```\\n\\n`pnpm test` already runs `pnpm build` befo

